    {
      "id": "launch-content-entry-path-audit-2026-08-16",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Customer Entry-Path Audit",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 1440,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 620,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "title": "Customer Entry-Path Audit — 2026-08-16",
        "auditStatus": "complete",
        "auditedRefs": [
          "github://twilite-zone/public/README.md",
          "github://twilite-zone/public/products/twilite/root.node",
          "github://mikemartinez1974/public/ideas/public-browsing-paid-production.node",
          "twilite/app/api/billing/create-checkout-session/route.js"
        ],
        "markdown": "# Customer Entry-Path Audit\n\n## Public README\nThe public README is only a repository label and pointer to `root.node`. It does not explain the audience, product value, free/paid boundary, price, trial, or next action.\n\n## Product graph\nThe flagship graph clearly explains persistent human-and-AI graph work, why structure matters, and several strong-fit audiences. It does not state the subscription offer, price, free public-browsing promise, one-month trial, or a direct signup path; its CTA routes to tutorial and product browsing.\n\n## Subscription offer\nThe product-policy graph contains a promising boundary: public graph browsing stays free while authenticated repository-backed production is paid. It lists concrete paid capabilities and proposes a bounded sandbox plus one-month full-product trial. The offer is still an internal idea with an open paywall question and untested willingness-to-pay assumption, not a customer-ready subscription surface.\n\n## Trial signup path\nThe account drawer can create/sign into a Twilite account and launch Stripe Checkout. The configured default is $15/month, and checkout returns to Twilite for entitlement confirmation. The checkout session does not set a Stripe trial period, the UI says `Upgrade with Stripe` rather than offering a trial, and no public product CTA leads directly into signup. Payments Readiness warns not to advertise the trial until entitlement and cancellation behavior are proven.\n\n## Current acquisition bottleneck\nTwilite has a compelling product thesis and a working paid checkout, but no single public path connects promise → concrete subscriber value → price/trial truth → account creation → checkout.\n\n## Immediate sequence\n1. Replace the placeholder README with a concise product entry and durable graph links.\n2. Add the free/paid boundary, $15/month offer, subscriber-value surface, and signup/trial CTA to the product graph.\n3. Turn the policy idea into the customer-facing What You Get When You Subscribe graph.\n4. Implement and smoke-test the one-month trial before publishing trial language.\n5. Then choose the first narrow customer profile and begin direct outreach.",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "launch-content-declaration"
        }
      }
    },
